---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const windows = String.raw`$openssl = 'C:\Program Files\FireDaemon OpenSSL 3.5\bin\openssl.exe'
& $openssl version
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'OpenSSL failed' }`;
const folder = String.raw`$folder = 'D:\Private Zodiac Keys' # choose a new, nonsynced local NTFS folder
if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $folder) { throw 'Choose a new folder; do not overwrite keys' }
$owner = [Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent().User
$acl = New-Object Security.AccessControl.DirectorySecurity
$acl.SetOwner($owner)
$acl.SetAccessRuleProtection($true, $false)
$rule = New-Object Security.AccessControl.FileSystemAccessRule($owner, 'FullControl', 'ContainerInherit,ObjectInherit', 'None', 'Allow')
$acl.AddAccessRule($rule)
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $folder -ErrorAction Stop | Out-Null
Set-Acl -LiteralPath $folder -AclObject $acl
Get-Acl -LiteralPath $folder | Format-List Owner,AccessToString,AreAccessRulesProtected
Set-Location -LiteralPath $folder`;
const commands = String.raw`# Every destination must be absent. Stop after any failed command.
if (Test-Path -LiteralPath '.\rsa-private-initial-encrypted.pem') { throw 'Already exists' }
& $openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -aes-256-cbc -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:4096 -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_pubexp:65537 -out '.\rsa-private-initial-encrypted.pem'
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Generation failed' }

if (Test-Path -LiteralPath '.\rsa-private-encrypted.pem') { throw 'Already exists' }
& $openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -in '.\rsa-private-initial-encrypted.pem' -out '.\rsa-private-encrypted.pem' -v2 aes-256-cbc -v2prf hmacWithSHA256 -iter 600000 -saltlen 16
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Rewrap failed' }

if (Test-Path -LiteralPath '.\rsa-public.pem') { throw 'Already exists' }
& $openssl pkey -in '.\rsa-private-encrypted.pem' -pubout -out '.\rsa-public.pem'
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Public extraction failed' }
& $openssl pkey -in '.\rsa-private-encrypted.pem' -check -noout
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Validation failed' }`;
const fingerprint = String.raw`# This DER file contains PUBLIC data only. Never substitute a private-key command.
if (Test-Path -LiteralPath '.\rsa-public.der') { throw 'Already exists' }
& $openssl pkey -pubin -in '.\rsa-public.pem' -outform DER -out '.\rsa-public.der'
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'Public conversion failed' }
(Get-FileHash -LiteralPath '.\rsa-public.der' -Algorithm SHA256).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()
Remove-Item -LiteralPath '.\rsa-public.der'
& 'C:\Verified Tools\zodiac-decrypt.exe' verify-key --key '.\rsa-private-encrypted.pem' --public '.\rsa-public.pem'`;
const unix = String.raw`# Key creation only; the v1 receiver currently supports Windows.
umask 077
mkdir -m 700 ./private-zodiac-keys && cd ./private-zodiac-keys
# Stop if mkdir fails. These outputs must not already exist.
openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -aes-256-cbc -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:4096 -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_pubexp:65537 -out rsa-private-initial-encrypted.pem
openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -in rsa-private-initial-encrypted.pem -out rsa-private-encrypted.pem -v2 aes-256-cbc -v2prf hmacWithSHA256 -iter 600000 -saltlen 16
openssl pkey -in rsa-private-encrypted.pem -pubout -out rsa-public.pem
openssl pkey -in rsa-private-encrypted.pem -check -noout
# Stop on each failure; never reuse a partially completed folder.`;
---

<Layout title="Keys">
  <h1>Create and keep recipient keys offline</h1>
  <p>
    Development software: use disposable test keys and nonsecret messages until
    reviewed, verified releases exist. Create keys on a trusted local machine.
    Only <strong>rsa-public.pem</strong> belongs in the sender. Private keys and
    passphrases never belong in this website, chat, support tickets or the
    repository.
  </p>
  <nav aria-label="Key setup steps">
    <a href="#install">Install</a> · <a href="#custody">Private folder</a> ·{' '}
    <a href="#generate">Create keys</a> · <a href="#verify">Verify</a> ·{' '}
    <a href="#backup">Backup and rotation</a>
  </nav>
  <h2 id="install">1. Verify OpenSSL before running it</h2>
  <p>
    On Windows use the latest patched supported FireDaemon OpenSSL 3.5 LTS.
    Checked 4 October 2026: upstream and vendor list 3.5.9; upstream supports
    3.5 until 8 April 2030. Recheck the{' '}
    <a href="https://openssl-library.org/source/">upstream releases</a>,{' '}
    <a href="https://openssl-library.org/news/vulnerabilities/">
      security advisories
    </a>{' '}
    and{' '}
    <a href="https://www.firedaemon.com/download-firedaemon-openssl">
      vendor downloads
    </a>{' '}
    when installing. Third-party packaging and version numbers alone do not
    establish trust.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the{' '}
    <a href="https://www.firedaemon.com/download-firedaemon-openssl-3-5-x64">
      direct vendor x64 installer
    </a>{' '}
    for x64 Windows. Check Settings → System → About for your architecture.
    ARM64 users need the vendor ARM64 ZIP and its documented layout; that setup
    and native receiver are not validated here. Avoid search-ad downloads. Do
    not enable a FIPS provider for this setup; this project makes no
    certification claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before execution, use Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 on the downloaded
    installer and compare its full hash with trusted current vendor information.
    The checked 3.5.9 x64 installer hash is{' '}
    <code>
      cecbaee4ae836693ae60e6e010fe374449fefde093b5dd8e93953b6657e4225d
    </code>
    . Use Get-AuthenticodeSignature: Status must be Valid and the publisher must
    be FireDaemon Technologies Limited, confirmed through the vendor. A hash
    copied from this site alone is not independent verification.
  </p>
  <p>
    Follow the verified installer wizard. Keep the default path or record your
    chosen path, then run the following in PowerShell. Expect OpenSSL 3.5.9 or
    the newer patched 3.5 release you verified. The installer payload’s bin
    executable and these commands were tested; a full system-wide wizard
    installation was not performed.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{windows}</code></pre>
  <h2 id="custody">2. Restrict access before creating keys</h2>
  <p>
    Choose a new folder on your local fixed NTFS disk, outside cloud sync,
    shared folders and the repository. Use ordinary directories without
    junctions, symlinks or mapped drives. Run the following in your own console
    with transcripts, recording and screen sharing disabled. Check that access
    is protected and only your current account has FullControl before
    continuing. Administrators and malware running as you can still access the
    files.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{folder}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Generate two independent random 24–32 character <strong>ASCII-only</strong>{' '}
    passphrases in a trusted local password manager: one temporary and one
    final. Eight characters is weak; predictable long phrases and complexity
    checklists do not establish entropy. Keep a protected recoverable passphrase
    backup separate from the encrypted file. Interactive Windows non-ASCII
    OpenSSL/receiver compatibility remains untested. Never put passwords in
    command arguments, environment variables, piped input or shell history.
  </p>
  <h2 id="generate">3. Create the encrypted pair once</h2>
  <p>
    The first command asks you to enter and confirm the temporary passphrase.
    The second unlocks that intermediate with the temporary passphrase, then
    asks you to enter and confirm the final passphrase. Public extraction and
    validation each ask for the final passphrase. Prompts hide input; enter it
    in the local console only. Substitute 3072 for 4096 if desired, keeping
    exponent 65537.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{commands}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Both private files have the ENCRYPTED PRIVATE KEY PEM header. The final file
    uses PBES2, PBKDF2 with explicit HMAC-SHA256, AES-256-CBC, 600,000
    iterations and a 16-byte salt. No plaintext private file is created. The
    intermediate has a weaker OpenSSL-default work factor: keep it out of sync
    and backups. The work factor slows password guessing; it does not prevent
    brute force or authenticate the encrypted key file. See the{' '}
    <a href="https://docs.openssl.org/3.5/man1/openssl-pkcs8/">
      OpenSSL conversion options
    </a>
    .
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>Unix-like key creation</summary>
    <p>
      Use a verified current OpenSSL 3.5 with -saltlen support, a nonsynced
      private directory and umask 077 before generation. Stop after any failure.
      These Unix commands are documented equivalents; this Windows development
      environment has not run the Unix permission trial.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{unix}</code></pre>
  </details>
  <h2 id="verify">4. Verify the pair and recipient identity</h2>
  <p>
    rsa-public.pem must have the PUBLIC KEY header (SPKI), not RSA PUBLIC KEY or
    a certificate. Compute SHA-256 over its DER SPKI bytes, not the PEM text.
    The receiver’s verify-key command asks for the final passphrase, compares
    the actual public half and reports only RSA size and fingerprint. Replace
    its example path with the separately verified executable.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{fingerprint}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Compare the complete 64-character lowercase fingerprint with the browser’s
    Recipient details and with the value exchanged through an independent
    trusted recipient channel. Local filenames and names are unverified labels.
    Select this public PEM in the sender, encrypt a nonsecret test message, and
    have the recipient decrypt and confirm the exact text. See{' '}
    <a href="/receive/">recipient readiness</a>. Encryption success alone does
    not prove possession of the private key.
  </p>
  <h2 id="backup">5. Restore a backup before removing the intermediate</h2>
  <p>
    Back up only the final encrypted private PEM and public PEM to a protected
    offline location. Keep the passphrase recoverable through a separate
    protected backup. Restore the encrypted backup into a second private
    nonsynced local folder, run verify-key against the original public PEM, and
    decrypt the test ciphertext to a new output name. Compare the restored
    fingerprint and exact test message. Check private-folder permissions on each
    copy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only after final validation, pair verification and that backup drill pass,
    remove the specific rsa-private-initial-encrypted.pem file. Never delete the
    final key. Deletion cannot promise erasure of SSD history, snapshots or
    retained copies; a recovered intermediate is still a way to attack the same
    RSA key. Keep encrypted private material outside source control and ordinary
    shared storage. Losing both the private key and its usable backup, or losing
    the passphrase, makes old messages unrecoverable by the website.
  </p>
  <p>
    For rotation, create a new pair in a new folder, verify its identity and
    fingerprint through the trusted channel, and complete a new readiness drill
    before distributing the replacement public key. Retain old encrypted private
    keys and passphrases securely for old ciphertext; a new key cannot decrypt
    old messages. Document which verified identity/fingerprint applies to each
    key without storing message history in the sender.
  </p>
</Layout>
